one of the great philosophers on whom I  do a good bit of scholarship and also  have taught a number of times is Thomas  Hobbes one of the I considered him  fathers of early modern philosophy a lot  of people would say that Descartes is  the father of modern philosophy but i  would say that Hobbes certainly deserves  to be called the second father of a new  way of doing things and although I am a  hob scholar in that I've you know  published quite a few pieces on Hobbs  over the years I'm not a Hobbesian that  is I'm not somebody who puts him fourth  as you know the way in which we ought to  view things as a matter of fact I'm  attracted to Hobbes in part and I'll  tell you more about this a bit later  precisely because I think that he shows  us how things could go drastically wrong  not only in his own times but in the  21st century and also as they did in the  20th century so I'm not a Hobbesian but  I am you could say in some respects of  Hobbes admirer but also somebody who  whose admiration is mixed with with fear  and you can tell just how much i read  and re-read Hobbes because this is my  every man's version of Leviathan I'm not  sure where the back bit went off to I  mean all the words are still in there  and admittedly I don't really read much  in part 3 and 4 and the Leviathan but  parts one and two you see they've gotten  quite a bit of use out of them there's a  ton of underlining and read underlining  in many different colors in here as well  I first started reading Hobbs you know I  think if I if I'm recalling this right  back as an undergraduate at Lakeland  College in a Lakeland University which  side note no longer has a philosophy  program there  lot of us in philosophy at the time but  I had to read him free a modern  philosophy class and I don't remember  being particularly impressed by most of  what I was reading it was in that thanh  anthology so it wasn't the totality of  Leviathan I did enjoy you know reading  his depiction of the state of nature i  found you know his diction a bit  difficult myself to to read but that was  just because i hadn't gotten used to his  own way of talking you know a lot of  times when we have ancient and early  modern authors in translation because  the translators will make them as  up-to-date as possible those  translations are sometimes a bit more  readable than you know 16th 17th 18th  century literature and so Hobbs suffered  a bit from that for for me at least as I  started you know thinking more and more  in terms of political philosophy and  reading the Leviathan as a systematic  document which is what Hobbes intended  for it not just looking at this chapter  or this chapter by itself but looking at  the totality of what he was putting  together I found myself becoming more  and more impressed by by Hobbes thought  he was somebody who was a staple I  taught him many times early on as a  matter of fact I think the very first  time that I got to teach him I was still  a graduate student I was teaching a  class on philosophy politics and justice  or perhaps the flaw maybe was philosophy  of politics law and justice or something  like that but Leviathan was something I  had my students read and then i would i  would actually use it in quite a few  ethics classes as well as you know  classes on 19th century philosophy or  intro classes where i wanted to get the  students to you know grapple with some  classic political philosophy and you  know i found myself you know struck by  by the scope of his thought and by its  implications particularly for conflict  in the  present so you know over the course of  time I ended up doing quite a bit of  presenting and and some of those  presentations get turned into articles  one of the ones that I had early on was  in this journal octa philosophic ah and  that's the laws of nature as moral norms  in Hobbes Leviathan that ended up being  turned into a forum because some of the  people who were involved with octa  philosophical thought that my reading of  Hobbes was a little bit too radical and  and they they disagreed with it so we  had a scholarly debate about that also  in octave philosophical i'll put links  to those that the article and then the  forum discussion about it here and then  i published another one in philosophy  and social criticism this one was called  reason is danger and remedy for the  modern subject in Hobbes Leviathan and  in it I was arguing that Hobbes is you  know particularly important to read  because he articulates this this  situation that were caught in and one of  the one of the ways in which people will  often view you know matters and then  sort of he provides a solution which is  a solution that many people have taken  and it's a solution ultimately of  totalitarianism Hobbes was was struck in  part by you know the need for social  order in watching an English Civil War  unfold and that English Civil War ended  up producing what's arguably one of the  first early totalitarian societies you  know thankfully a fairly short one you  know and Hobbes is is writing you know  in response that you can take out a lot  of his sort of you know references to  you know early modern politics and the  ins and outs of the English scene and  apply it in a much larger so  and you can get this this sort of look  it's either Anarchy or it's a poorly  functioning society in which there  really isn't any justice and it's just  force and you know things are going to  fall apart sooner or later or they're  just going to keep on going through and  new elites are going to replace old  elites engaging an ideological conflict  with each other or you can have this  well ordered society of the sort that  I'm setting out which by the way is  going to have to be enforced by  authority which will extend not only to  you know what you do but what you think  or what you teach Hobbes thinks that all  of this can be set out and this is  something that's very revolutionary  about Hobbes he thinks all of this can  be set out in a form of a new social or  political science what he would call  moral science right and this has been an  attractive prospect for many people I  would you know a lot of people want to  say you know that Nietzsche should be  the person that we look to about how  things could go wrong and all that I  actually think that Hobbes is in a  certain respect more apropos more  influential even in indirect ways than  the Nietzsche is he shows us how these  dilemmas can work out so one of the  other things that I wrote which never  ended up getting published was for a  volume that a colleague was working on  and it had to do with contemporary  African politics and how to avoid  falling into Hobbesian dilemmas because  when there is not a strong civil society  and there is a history of you know  revolts and power struggles and not only  you know in terms of like the political  system but you might call it the  cultural or ideological system also gets  involved so there's no way to take a  objective although everybody saying  their objective there's no way to take a  purely objective stance it's it's easy  to see why things that look similar to  Hobbs's Commonwealth would be where  things would go and I think  that that usually can't be realized  totalitarian societies are in many  respects even worse than the the  anarchic breaking down unfair unjust  societies that they they are used to  replace but you can see why that sort of  thing would be would be attractive so I  think that and I still think this today  I just haven't had the time to much  follow-up scholarship on hubs I think  that Hobbes is actually outlining a  fundamental human problematic one that  becomes particularly acute in in  modernity and continues through so I  brank him actually up you know in in the  thinkers of the first rate and I look at  this book Leviathan as one of the great  works of Western philosophy that that  everybody should read I do have a few  other things I've published here and  there on Hobbs 11 paper that I I don't  know if I'll ever get it reworked and  sent out for publication and hobbes and  there was a discussion about could he  actually be a virtue at the system I  said well he does talk about virtue but  it if he is a virtue ethicist it's in a  very different way than then other  virtue ethicists would would you know  understand that to be but maybe i don't  all see if i'll send out sometime right  now I consider it to be an orphaned  paper but I did publish another piece  about the five states of nature in  Hobbes Leviathan I think that there's  there's a number of different ways that  he construes the the famous state of  nature and I provide an exegetical  argument for that so i'll put a link to  that as well you might find that  interesting i gave that to a new online  journal that was looking for for papers  so in any case that that's a little bit  about why i think people should study  thomas hobbes well I think his ideas are  worth examining and thinking through  again I don't I'm not endorsing him  I don't I don't agree with him as a  matter of fact he kind of makes me  worried as I read through the stuff but  but I have a kind of fascination with  this mindset that he articulates and how  well he's able to address a lot of  issues that that come up and so I see  him as somebody whose whose ideas  whether you know coming directly from  Hobbs or formulated in other ways  because other people can arrive at  similar ideas will be attractive to many  people in situations of intractable  social and political conflict in  ideological conflict and so I think it's  very important to to look at at him and  to think about how we would move past  this where the weak points in his  arguments are but you know all that said  I really enjoy working at him I really  enjoy teaching him and I probably will  continue to do both of those over the  course of my career
 

